[0m[0m
The C is one small file, so the translation is a near-direct mapping:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mfma_array[0m and [38;5;10mdriver[0m are both non-static in the C, so both are exported. No namespace macros exist in [38;5;10mdriver.h[0m, so the linker symbols are plain [38;5;10mdriver[0m and [38;5;10mfma_array[0m — confirmed with [38;5;10mnm[0m.[0m[0m
- [38;5;10minner[0m is [38;5;10mstatic[0m, so it stays a private Rust [38;5;10mfn[0m.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Three points where the mapping needed care:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;10mfma_array[0m is called as [38;5;10mfma_array(out, out, out, out, len)[0m — all four pointers alias. I kept a raw-pointer loop that re-reads each input per iteration rather than using slices, so the store-then-read ordering stays observable for callers that pass overlapping arrays. Signed overflow is UB in C but wraps on real targets, so [38;5;10mwrapping_mul[0m/[38;5;10mwrapping_add[0m reproduce what the compiled C does; [38;5;10m46341*46341[0m and [38;5;10mINT_MIN[0m/[38;5;10mINT_MAX[0m cases are in the test set above.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Printing goes through the C library's [38;5;10mprintf[0m, not Rust's [38;5;10mprintln![0m. Since this is a shared library likely loaded alongside C code, using the same stdio stream preserves buffering and interleaving, not just the digits.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;10mint out[len][0m is a VLA; I used a [38;5;10mVec[0m instead. One deliberate divergence: for [38;5;10mlen < 0[0m the C computes [38;5;10mlen * sizeof(int)[0m, which converts to a huge [38;5;10msize_t[0m and makes [38;5;10mmemcpy[0m crash. I clamp to an empty buffer instead of replicating the crash. The subsequent loops are no-ops either way, so behavior only differs on an input that would segfault the original.[0m[0m
